WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.427
Cardinality describes how many rows can match a key.

00:00:03.427 --> 00:00:09.362
In a one-to-one relationship, each key matches at most one row on both sides.

00:00:09.362 --> 00:00:11.012
Those joins are usually easy to reason about.

00:00:11.012 --> 00:00:15.197
In a one-to-many relationship, one patient may match several admissions.

00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:21.887
The patient information repeats once for each admission, and that can be perfectly correct.

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:24.252
The risk appears when we join several one-to-many child tables at the same time.

00:00:24.252 --> 00:00:29.482
Suppose one patient has two admissions, two lab tests, and two prescriptions.

00:00:29.482 --> 00:00:35.497
If all three tables are joined independently by patient_id, we can create two times two times two, or eight,

00:00:35.497 --> 00:00:39.547
combinations. This idea applies everywhere.

00:00:39.547 --> 00:00:44.592
One customer can have many orders, many support tickets, and many website sessions.

00:00:44.592 --> 00:00:49.827
One student can have many courses, many submissions, and many advising visits.

00:00:49.827 --> 00:00:56.207
If you join all of those detail tables together without thinking about grain, counts can become inflated.

00:00:56.207 --> 00:00:59.792
Before aggregating, predict the relationship type for each join.

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:04.887
If the final result should be one row per parent, consider summarizing each child table to the parent grain

00:01:04.887 --> 00:01:06.590
before joining.
